---
import '../../styles/pages.css';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Arrow from '../../components/Arrow.astro';
import Draft from '../../components/Draft.astro';
import PageHead from '../../components/PageHead.astro';
import { LINKEDIN_URL, NAV, REPOSITORIES, TOOL_LINKS, X_URL, type NavGroup } from '../../lib/site';
import { aboutPageNode, founderNode } from '../../lib/structured-data';

/**
 * What is on the site today, taken from the main menu and the list of tools, so this page changes
 * when a section or a tool is added and cannot fall behind the rest of the site.
 */
const groups: { label: string; home: string | undefined; items: readonly { href: string; label: string; note?: string }[] }[] = [
  ...NAV.filter((entry): entry is NavGroup => 'items' in entry).map((g) => ({ label: g.label, home: g.home, items: g.items })),
  { label: 'Tools', home: '/tools/', items: TOOL_LINKS },
];
// A tool page with no line in TOOL_LINKS stops the build here.
const toolPages = Object.keys(import.meta.glob('../tools/*/index.astro')).length;
if (toolPages !== TOOL_LINKS.length) throw new Error(`The site has ${toolPages} tool pages but TOOL_LINKS in src/lib/site.ts lists ${TOOL_LINKS.length}`);
---

<Base
  title="About: the mission and founder Will Berchtold"
  description="Why ClinX exists, what it publishes, how it works and who builds it: Will Berchtold, founder of ClinX and ReferralFinderX. How it stays independent and how to report a correction."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'About', path: '/about/' }]}
  about={[aboutPageNode(), founderNode()]}
>
  <PageHead title="About" trail={[{ label: 'ClinX' }]}>
    <p class="lede">
      ClinX exists to put capable healthcare tools and data within reach of every clinic, whatever its size or budget.
    </p>
  </PageHead>

  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="why">
    <h2 id="why">Why it exists</h2>
    <div class="split-body">
      <p class="head-tag"><Draft /></p>
      <p>
        In healthcare, the most capable tools are gated behind six-figure contracts, complex IT projects and compliance burdens. A large health system can take those on. Most clinics cannot, so they go without resources that would help them get better outcomes for their patients.
      </p>
      <p>
        ClinX is built to close that gap: open data that anyone can check and reuse, and tools a clinic can adopt without a large contract or a long project.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="here">
    <h2 id="here">What is here</h2>
    <div class="split-body">
      <p>
        So far the data covers the federal 340B drug program and the federally funded health centers, and the tools work on the reports a clinic already has. Everything on this site is free to use.
      </p>
      {
        groups.map((g) => (
          <>
            <h3 class="label here-label">{g.home ? <a href={g.home}>{g.label}</a> : g.label}</h3>
            <ul class="repos">
              {g.items.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <strong><a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a></strong>
                  <span>{item.note}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        ))
      }
      <p>Every table can be downloaded from the <a href="/data/">Data</a> page, and an AI assistant can <a href="/data/#assistants">read the same tables</a>. The articles and guides are under <a href="/writing/">Writing</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="rules">
    <h2 id="rules">How it works</h2>
    <div class="split-body">
      <ol class="rules">
        <li>
          <strong>As filed</strong>
          <p>Every figure is copied from a public record and links to the page or file it came from. Nothing is corrected or estimated. Where a filing's own figures differ, both are shown.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Shown, not judged</strong>
          <p>The site says what was filed and how each measure is calculated. It does not say why a figure is what it is.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Open</strong>
          <p>The data and the code are free to use, to check and to build on.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="btn-row"><a class="btn ghost" href="/methods/">Read the methods <Arrow /></a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="founder" aria-labelledby="founder-name">
    <div class="founder-id">
      <div class="founder-top">
        <picture class="founder-photo">
          <source type="image/webp" srcset="/founder/will-berchtold-480.webp 480w, /founder/will-berchtold-960.webp 960w" sizes="136px" />
          <img
            src="/founder/will-berchtold-480.jpg"
            srcset="/founder/will-berchtold-480.jpg 480w, /founder/will-berchtold-960.jpg 960w"
            sizes="136px"
            width="480"
            height="596"
            alt="Portrait of Will Berchtold."
          />
        </picture>
        <div>
          <p class="label">Founder</p>
          <h2 id="founder-name">Will Berchtold</h2>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl>
        <div>
          <dt>Founder of</dt>
          <dd>ClinX and <a href="/products/">ReferralFinderX</a></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Works in</dt>
          <dd>Community health</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Based in</dt>
          <dd>Rhode Island</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn" href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>
        <a class="btn" href={X_URL}>X</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="founder-say">
      <p>ClinX is built by Will Berchtold, alongside his work in community health.</p>
      <p>
        He trained in computer architecture and programming, and built his career in operations. At McKesson he supervised inventory quality control and coordinated pharmacy and hospital inventory changeovers across the eastern United States. He also spent more than a decade in restaurants, from the kitchen to sushi chef to manager.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since 2022 he has worked in community health in Rhode Island, in health informatics, on the referral desk and in patient access. He is also the founder of ReferralFinderX, a directory of specialist offices for referral teams.
      </p>
      <p class="head-tag"><Draft /></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="independence">
    <h2 id="independence">Independence</h2>
    <div class="split-body">
      <p class="head-tag"><Draft /></p>
      <p>
        Will works at a Rhode Island community health center, and health centers are among the organizations whose public reports are shown here. ClinX is his own project. It is not connected to his employer and does not speak for it.
      </p>
      <p>Every organization's figures are shown the same way: as filed or as published, with a link to the source.</p>
      <p>The open data and the tools on this site are free and stand apart from anything ClinX sells. They do not depend on <a href="/products/">ReferralFinderX</a>, and ReferralFinderX does not depend on them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="corrections">
    <h2 id="corrections">Report a correction</h2>
    <div class="split-body">
      <p>If a figure here does not match the source it cites, please report it.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Name the page on ClinX and the figure. For a 340B filing, name the schedule and the line.</li>
        <li>Say what the site shows and what the source prints.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The source governs. A confirmed correction is released as a new version of the data and listed in its changelog.</p>
      <p class="head-tag"><span>Corrections go through <a href={`${REPOSITORIES.dataset}/issues`}>the 340B dataset's public issue list</a>, or the <a href="/contact/">contact form</a>.</span> <Draft /></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split" aria-labelledby="licences">
    <h2 id="licences">Licences</h2>
    <div class="split-body">
      <p>
        The code is under the MIT licence. The data is under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (CC BY 4.0). The filings and files themselves are public records of the state or federal agency that published them.
      </p>
      <p>The names ClinX and ReferralFinderX and their logos are not covered by those licences.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
